    \nextpage vscodeext-how-tos.html

    \ingroup vscodeext-tutorials

    \title Tutorial: Qt Bridge for C#

    \brief Create a Qt Bridge for C# project with a project wizard that uses
    Qt Quick Controls.

    This tutorial illustrates how to build fluid animated user interfaces in QML
    with \l{Qt Quick}, while keeping application logic, models, and domain code
    in C#.

    The Qt Quick module provides all the basic types necessary for creating user
    interfaces with QML. It provides a visual canvas and includes types for
    creating and animating visual components, receiving user input, and so on.

    This tutorial describes the example application that the
    \uicontrol {New project} wizard generates. It imports QML types from the
    Qt Quick, \l{Qt Quick Controls}, and \l{Qt Quick Layouts} modules. It
    also creates a C# entry point and .NET project file for building and running
    the application the .NET way.

    \image {vscodeext-tutorial-quick-csharp.webp} {Qt Quick C# application}

    For more examples of creating Qt for C# applications, see
    \l{Qt Bridge for C#}.

    \section1 Before you start

    Before you start, you have to:

    \list
        \li \l {Installation}{Install \QVSC}
        \li \l {Register Qt installations}
    \endlist

    \section1 Create a Qt Quick C# application project

    To create a Qt Quick application with C# code in Visual Studio Code:

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Welcome}, and then select \uicontrol {New project}.
        \li In \uicontrol {Project}, select
            \uicontrol {Qt Quick application (Qt for C#)}.
            \image {vscodeext-create-new-project-quick-csharp.webp} {A Qt Quick C# application in the New item view}
        \li In \uicontrol {Name}, enter the project name.
        \li In \uicontrol {Create in}, enter the path for the project files.
        \li Select \c {Sample code} to add a small \c {Counter} C# class that QML
            can see, and to extend \c {Main.qml} with a labeled button. Use it to
            explore how a C# object and a QML type connect. Remove the class once
            you start building your own UI.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Open in new window} to create the project files
            and open them in a new VS Code window.
    \endlist

    You now have a small working Qt Quick application with Qt for C# code.

    The wizard creates the following files:

    \list
        \li \c {MyQtApp.csproj}, which is a regular .NET project file. It
            references the Qt Bridge for C# package for your platform and
            includes \c {Program.cs} and \c {Main.qml} as project items.
            You build and run it the same way you would any other .NET project,
            with \c {dotnet build} and \c {dotnet run} or from your editor.
        \li \c {Program.cs}, which is the C# entry point. It loads the QML code
            and keeps the application running until the QML window closes.
        \li \c {Main.qml}, which is the first QML file the application loads. It
            describes the window and the UI that appear when the application
            starts.
    \endlist

    \section1 Run the application

    The main view of the application shows an application window with background
    color, text, and a button. Selecting the button updates a counter for the
    number of times the button was clicked.

    To build and run the application, enter the following command in
    \uicontrol Terminal:

    \code [text]
    dotnet run
    \endcode

    \note After you build the application once, the code completion offers more
    complete suggestions for C# classes.

    \note To preview a QML file without building the application, select
    \inlineimage {preview-file.png} {Preview current QML file live} on
    the editor toolbar

    \section1 Inspect the QML code

    Open \c Main.qml to inspect the generated QML code.

    \section2 QML imports

    The file has import statements for the Qt modules that contain the QML
    types used in the application:

    \code [text]
    import QtQuick
    import QtQuick.Controls
    import QtQuick.Layouts
    \endcode

    \section2 Main window

    The file contains an instance of the \l{ApplicationWindow} QML type, which
    creates a main window. The window properties set an ID, initial and minimum
    size, background color, and a window title. Also, they determine that the
    window is visible on the screen.

    \code [text]
    ApplicationWindow {
        id: win
        visible: true
        title: "Qt Bridge for C#"
        width: 365; height: 510
        color: "#121212"
    \endcode

    \section2 Qt Quick Layouts

    The window contains an instance of the \l {ColumnLayout} QML type from the
    Qt Quick Layouts module, which positions the window contents in a column.

    \code [text]
        ColumnLayout {
            anchors.fill: parent
            anchors.margins: 24
            spacing: 20
    \endcode

    \section2 Qt Quick Controls

    The Qt Quick Controls module provides a set of controls for building complete
    user interfaces in Qt Quick. The application contains instaces of Label and
    Button controls.

    \section3 Label

    The \l [QML] {Label} properties set the heading text, as well as its color
    and its size in pixels. Also, they center the label text horizontally and
    vertically within the element's width and height.

    The Layout properties make the label fit the column cell with 24 pixel
    margins and 20 pixel spacing.

    \code [text]
            Label {
                text: "Hello, World!"
                font.pixelSize: 40
                color: "white"
                horizontalAlignment: Text.AlignHCenter
                Layout.alignment: Qt.AlignHCenter
            }
    \endcode

    \section3 Button

    The \l [QtQuickControls] {Button} \c text property sets the button text
    and adds a counter for the button clicks.

    The Layout properties make the button fit the column cell with 24 pixel
    margins and 20 pixel spacing. They set the preferred button height at 48
    pixels.

    The font property sets the text size to 16 pixels.

    \code [text]
        Button {
            id: counterBtn
            text: Counter.clicks === 0 ? "Click me" : "Clicked " + Counter.clicks + " times"

            Layout.fillWidth: true
            Layout.preferredHeight: 48
            font.pixelSize: 16

            hoverEnabled: false
            focusPolicy: Qt.NoFocus
        ...
        }
    \endcode

    The custom \c color property sets the button color to Qt green.

    The \c {background} item holds an instance of the \l [QML] {Rectangle} type.
    The rectangle properties make the button corners round and set the button
    background color to a darker shade of Qt green when it is clicked.

    \code [text]
            ...
            readonly property color brandGreen: "#41cd52"

            background: Rectangle {
                radius: 12
                border.width: 0
                color: counterBtn.down
                    ? Qt.darker(counterBtn.brandGreen, 1.2)
                    : counterBtn.brandGreen
            }
    \endcode

    The \l [QML] {Text} properties bind the button text, as well as its color and
    font to the window mode.

    \code [text]
            contentItem: Text {
                text: counterBtn.text
                color: "white"
                font: counterBtn.font
                horizontalAlignment: Text.AlignHCenter
                verticalAlignment: Text.AlignVCenter
                elide: Text.ElideRight
            }

            onClicked: Counter.clicks += 1
        }
    \endcode

    \section1 Inspect the C# entry point code

    Open the \c {Program.cs} file to inspect the generated C# code.

    The wizard adds the \c {CounterService} class, exposes it to QML, and
    references it from \c {Main.qml}.

    \code [C#]
    using Qt.MetaObject;
    using Qt.Quick;

    using System.ComponentModel;
    using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

    namespace MyQtApp;

    [QObject]
    [QmlElement(Name = "Counter", Singleton = true)]
    public class CounterService : INotifyPropertyChanged
    {
        public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;

        private int _clicks = 0;
        public int Clicks
        {
            get => _clicks;
            set
            {
                if (_clicks == value)
                    return;
                _clicks = value;
                OnPropertyChanged();
            }
        }

        protected virtual void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string? name = null)
            => PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(name));
    }
    \endcode

    The \c {Program} class loads the the QML code and keeps the application
    running until the QML window closes.

    \c {Qml.LoadFromRootModule("Main")} loads \c {Main.qml} as the application's
    root QML file and creates the window it describes.

    \c {Qml.WaitForExit()} blocks \c {Main} until the QML side shuts down, so the
    process stays alive while the UI runs.

    \code [C#]
    public class Program
    {
        internal static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Qml.LoadFromRootModule("Main");
            Qml.WaitForExit();
        }
    }
    \endcode

    Usually, you do not need to change either call for a project.

    \section1 Inspect the .NET project file

    Open the \c {MyQtApp.csproj} file to inspect the .NET project file. It
    references the Qt Bridge for C# package for your platform and includes
    \c {Program.cs} and \c {Main.qml} as project items.

    \code [text]
    <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

      <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
        <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
        <QtBridgePackagePrefix>QtGroup.Qt.Bridge.CSharp</QtBridgePackagePrefix>
        <QtBridgeTemplateArch Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateArch)' == ''"
          >$([System.Runtime.InteropServices.RuntimeInformation]::OSArchitecture)</QtBridgeTemplateArch>
        <QtBridgeTemplateArch Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateArch)' == 'X64'">x64</QtBridgeTemplateArch>
        <QtBridgeTemplateArch Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateArch)' == 'Arm64'">arm64</QtBridgeTemplateArch>
        <QtBridgeTemplateRid Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' == '' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''"
          >$(RuntimeIdentifier)</QtBridgeTemplateRid>
        <QtBridgeTemplateRid Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' == '' and $([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))"
          >win-x64</QtBridgeTemplateRid>
        <QtBridgeTemplateRid Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' == '' and $([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Linux'))"
          >linux-$(QtBridgeTemplateArch)</QtBridgeTemplateRid>
        <QtBridgePackageId>$(QtBridgePackagePrefix).$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)</QtBridgePackageId>
      </PropertyGroup>

      <ItemGroup Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' == 'win-x64' or '$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' == 'linux-x64'">
        <PackageReference Include="$(QtBridgePackageId)" Version="0.3.*-*" />
      </ItemGroup>

      <Target Name="EnsureSupportedQtBridgePlatform" BeforeTargets="Restore;Build"
              Condition="'$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' != 'win-x64' and '$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)' != 'linux-x64'">
        <Error Text="Qt Bridge for C# currently supports win-x64 and linux-x64 only. Selected RID: '$(QtBridgeTemplateRid)'." />
      </Target>

      <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="Main.qml"/>
        <Compile Include="Program.cs" />
      </ItemGroup>

    </Project>
    \endcode
*/
